1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,640
THEME SONG: In the not too
 distant future, next Sunday AD,

2
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:13,460
there was a guy named Joel, not
 too different from you or me.

3
00:00:13,460 --> 00:00:17,420
He worked at Gizmonic
 Institute, just another face

4
00:00:17,420 --> 00:00:19,550
in a red jump suit.

5
00:00:19,550 --> 00:00:22,390
He did a good job
 cleaning up the place,

6
00:00:22,390 --> 00:00:28,790
but his bosses didn't like him
 so they shot him into space!

7
00:00:28,790 --> 00:00:31,750
We'll send him cheesy movies.

8
00:00:31,750 --> 00:00:34,540
The worst we can find.

9
00:00:34,540 --> 00:00:35,380
La, la, la!

10
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:38,040
He'll have to sit
 and watch them all,

11
00:00:38,040 --> 00:00:40,780
then we'll monitor his mind.

12
00:00:40,780 --> 00:00:41,280
La, la, la.

13
00:00:41,280 --> 00:00:44,450
Now keep in mind
 Joel can't control

14
00:00:44,450 --> 00:00:46,850
where the movies begin or end.

15
00:00:46,850 --> 00:00:47,350
La, la, la.

16
00:00:47,350 --> 00:00:50,690
Because he used
 those special parts

17
00:00:50,690 --> 00:00:53,080
to make his robot friends.

18
00:00:55,960 --> 00:00:57,270
Robot roll call-- let's go.

19
00:00:57,270 --> 00:00:57,770
Cambot.

20
00:00:57,770 --> 00:00:58,740
Pan left.

21
00:00:58,740 --> 00:00:59,240
Gypsy.

22
00:00:59,240 --> 00:01:00,110
Hi, girl.

23
00:01:00,110 --> 00:01:00,610
Tom Servo.

24
00:01:00,610 --> 00:01:01,700
What a cool guy.

25
00:01:01,700 --> 00:01:02,200
Croooooow.

26
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:03,030
Oh, the wisecracker.

27
00:01:03,030 --> 00:01:06,080
If you're wondering how
 he eats and breathes,

28
00:01:06,080 --> 00:01:09,760
and other science
 facts-- la, la, la-- then

29
00:01:09,760 --> 00:01:11,970
repeat to yourself
 it's just a show,

30
00:01:11,970 --> 00:01:30,650
I should really just relax-- for
 "Mystery Science Theater 3000."

31
00:01:30,650 --> 00:01:32,340
-Oh, hi, everybody.

32
00:01:32,340 --> 00:01:33,720
Welcome to the
 Satellite of Love.

33
00:01:33,720 --> 00:01:34,500
I'm Joel Robinson.

34
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:36,560
And I was just teaching
 Tom Servo, here,

35
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:38,330
how to do macrame so
 he could get a head

36
00:01:38,330 --> 00:01:39,670
start on some of his
 Christmas presents.

37
00:01:39,670 --> 00:01:40,400
-OK, help me out here.

38
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:41,670
-OK, now it's up and over.

39
00:01:41,670 --> 00:01:42,790
And on the left side--
 -Up, over, left.

40
00:01:42,790 --> 00:01:44,270
- --and over, and cross
 over to the middle.

41
00:01:44,270 --> 00:01:44,760
-Cross over.
 -Ahem.

42
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:45,490
Knock knock!

43
00:01:45,490 --> 00:01:45,990
-Oh.

44
00:01:45,990 --> 00:01:47,330
Oh, hi, Crow.

45
00:01:47,330 --> 00:01:48,520
-Good morning, sir.

46
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:51,490
Uh, may I speak to the
 man or lady of the house?

47
00:01:51,490 --> 00:01:52,190
-Oh, boy.

48
00:01:52,190 --> 00:01:53,690
-Ah, hello ma'am.

49
00:01:53,690 --> 00:01:55,130
My name is Crow T Robot.

50
00:01:55,130 --> 00:01:58,130
And I'm selling grit to raise
 money to keep kids like me

51
00:01:58,130 --> 00:01:58,940
off the street.

52
00:01:58,940 --> 00:02:00,880
-Oh, not again, Crow!

53
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:01,880
-You need not pay now.

54
00:02:01,880 --> 00:02:03,980
You can simply place
 an order with me,

55
00:02:03,980 --> 00:02:05,770
and I will personally
 deliver your grit

56
00:02:05,770 --> 00:02:07,350
within four to six weeks.

57
00:02:07,350 --> 00:02:10,400
Or if you'd prefer, I
 do have some free grit,

58
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:13,280
which is complimentary
 to you at no charge.

59
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:14,380
-Crow, this is grit.

60
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:16,310
You're supposed to be
 selling "Grit" there,

61
00:02:16,310 --> 00:02:17,740
our nation's newspaper.

62
00:02:17,740 --> 00:02:19,060
This is true grit.

63
00:02:19,060 --> 00:02:20,020
-Uh, uh, uh.

64
00:02:20,020 --> 00:02:20,710
Oh.

65
00:02:20,710 --> 00:02:22,210
Uh, OK, sir.

66
00:02:22,210 --> 00:02:25,000
Uh, may I interest you
 and the little lady

67
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,860
in decorator driftwood candles,
 sure to complement any decor.

68
00:02:27,860 --> 00:02:28,360
-Come on, Crow!

69
00:02:28,360 --> 00:02:30,840
I already have four dozen
 turkey pizzas in the freezer.

70
00:02:30,840 --> 00:02:32,830
And you never even went on
 that band trip to Winnipeg.

71
00:02:32,830 --> 00:02:33,330
-I got sick.

72
00:02:33,330 --> 00:02:35,330
-Yeah, and besides, you
 know those watermelon seeds

73
00:02:35,330 --> 00:02:37,560
you sold me to buy
 a transistor radio?

74
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:38,760
They don't even work up here.

75
00:02:38,760 --> 00:02:41,310
And I've got about a
 kajillion Olympic good luck

76
00:02:41,310 --> 00:02:42,890
on your confirmation cards.

77
00:02:42,890 --> 00:02:45,070
-Well, I'm just trying to put
 a little jingle in my jeans.

78
00:02:45,070 --> 00:02:45,800
-Yeah, well, listen.

79
00:02:45,800 --> 00:02:46,900
I'm going to put
 a stop to you're

80
00:02:46,900 --> 00:02:48,450
sending away for stuff to sell.

81
00:02:48,450 --> 00:02:49,700
It's for your own good.

82
00:02:49,700 --> 00:02:50,800
-Uh, Joel, uh, let me ask you.

83
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:52,920
Uh, have you reviewed
 your insurance needs

84
00:02:52,920 --> 00:02:53,840
within the last year?

85
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:54,340
-Uh-oh.

86
00:02:54,340 --> 00:02:56,840
-Have you considered what
 would happen to your family

87
00:02:56,840 --> 00:02:58,070
if anything should
 happen to you?

88
00:02:58,070 --> 00:02:58,570
-Crow.

89
00:02:58,570 --> 00:02:59,510
-Uh, b-- what?

90
00:02:59,510 --> 00:03:01,370
You're telling me you
 don't love your family?

91
00:03:01,370 --> 00:03:01,870
Uh--

92
00:03:01,870 --> 00:03:03,030
-We'll be right back.

93
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:10,100
-Wow!

94
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:12,850
It says here, if I sell
 50 pounds of kitty litter,

95
00:03:12,850 --> 00:03:15,760
uh, I can win a shoestring
 and a picture of Eve Arden.

96
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:16,260
-Yeah.

97
00:03:16,260 --> 00:03:18,220
It says here, if
 I sell my soul, I

98
00:03:18,220 --> 00:03:20,110
can get a job working
 for Pat Buchanan.

99
00:03:20,110 --> 00:03:21,700
-Eh, cut the comedy, you two.

100
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:23,920
Uh, Governor and JJ are calling.

101
00:03:23,920 --> 00:03:26,170
-Well, Boobie, our
 experiment this week

102
00:03:26,170 --> 00:03:28,700
was supposed to be about
 brushing after meals,

103
00:03:28,700 --> 00:03:30,300
but this one here
 never stops eating!

104
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:31,220
-Pwah!

105
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:32,170
Ugh!

106
00:03:32,170 --> 00:03:32,800
Excuse me.

107
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:36,410
Uh, you know, for years
 now, Dr. Forrester

108
00:03:36,410 --> 00:03:39,540
has been trying to get me
 to not brush after eating.

109
00:03:39,540 --> 00:03:44,110
He's tried everything, pleading,
 scolding, electroshock therapy,

110
00:03:44,110 --> 00:03:45,610
attaching a clothes pin to my--

111
00:03:45,610 --> 00:03:47,270
-That's enough, Frank.

112
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:51,110
I, Dr. Clayton Forrester have
 discovered an important truth.

113
00:03:51,110 --> 00:03:54,420
Before adulthood, when
 booze becomes a stable,

114
00:03:54,420 --> 00:03:57,950
sugar is the next best
 substitute to parental love.

115
00:03:57,950 --> 00:04:01,230
That's why I've invented
 Deep 13 toothpaste.

116
00:04:01,230 --> 00:04:04,590
It's the candy coated,
 sugar frosted denta-frise,

117
00:04:04,590 --> 00:04:08,950
which 9 out of 10 disreputable
 dentists choose by name.

118
00:04:08,950 --> 00:04:11,800
-I'm having Chocolate Peanut
 Butter Fudge toothpaste.

119
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:15,780
It also comes in Cookies 'N
 Cream, Rocky Road, Banana Fudge

120
00:04:15,780 --> 00:04:17,420
Ripple, and 31 other flavors.

121
00:04:17,420 --> 00:04:18,010
Um.

122
00:04:18,010 --> 00:04:20,130
-Yes, now kids can
 brush their teeth

123
00:04:20,130 --> 00:04:22,550
and still rot their teeth.

124
00:04:22,550 --> 00:04:25,310
-Now, how to get kids to smoke.

125
00:04:25,310 --> 00:04:29,770
-At long last, Senator, have
 you no sense of decency?

126
00:04:29,770 --> 00:04:32,250
-Speaking of rotting
 teeth, our invention

127
00:04:32,250 --> 00:04:35,160
is inspired by big
 rock stars of our time.

128
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:35,660
-That's right.

129
00:04:35,660 --> 00:04:37,330
In the past, the
 only musicians who

130
00:04:37,330 --> 00:04:39,460
could afford to destroy
 their guitars every night

131
00:04:39,460 --> 00:04:42,960
were rich rock stars, like
 Pete Townsend, Jimi Hendrix,

132
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:43,950
Rick Springfield--

133
00:04:43,950 --> 00:04:45,520
-Uh, uh, Rick Springfield?

134
00:04:45,520 --> 00:04:47,300
-Well, I wish he'd
 destroy his guitar.

135
00:04:47,300 --> 00:04:47,800
Ha.

136
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:50,090
Anyway, now even been
 struggling garage bands

137
00:04:50,090 --> 00:04:53,400
can destroy their guitars with
 the new Rock 'N Wreck guitar.

138
00:04:53,400 --> 00:04:56,060
-Yeah, now you can unleash
 a glorious moment of energy!

139
00:04:56,060 --> 00:04:57,900
-Whoo-hoo!

140
00:04:57,900 --> 00:04:58,400
Ha, ha, ha.

141
00:04:58,400 --> 00:05:00,190
-But it doesn't have
 to be permanent.

142
00:05:00,190 --> 00:05:01,590
-Ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha.

143
00:05:01,590 --> 00:05:02,310
That's right.

144
00:05:02,310 --> 00:05:04,150
Most music careers
 may be temporary,

145
00:05:04,150 --> 00:05:06,500
but your guitar
 doesn't have to be.

146
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:08,300
-And as you spend the
 remainder of your life

147
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:10,820
working long hours
 at a fast food

148
00:05:10,820 --> 00:05:12,870
joint, folding
 cheese-a-ritos, you'll

149
00:05:12,870 --> 00:05:15,690
always have a souvenir of
 your once hopeful youth.

150
00:05:15,690 --> 00:05:16,950
-Really dark, Crow.

151
00:05:16,950 --> 00:05:19,440
-What do you think, sirs?

152
00:05:19,440 --> 00:05:20,260
-Frank, give me that!

153
00:05:20,260 --> 00:05:21,950
You've had enough!

154
00:05:21,950 --> 00:05:23,170
-Hey!

155
00:05:23,170 --> 00:05:25,450
Well, Boobie, uh,
 Rocky Jones, Winky,

156
00:05:25,450 --> 00:05:27,700
and the whole lethargic
 gang is back again,

157
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:30,280
in your experiment today,
 in the long awaited

158
00:05:30,280 --> 00:05:33,150
sequel to "Manhunt in
 Space," "Crash of the Moons,"

159
00:05:33,150 --> 00:05:35,780
plus a "General Hospital" short.

160
00:05:35,780 --> 00:05:36,660
Need I say more?

161
00:05:36,660 --> 00:05:38,100
-Hah!

162
00:05:38,100 --> 00:05:41,330
-Frank, quit bogarting
 that toothpaste.

163
00:05:41,330 --> 00:05:41,830
-Whoa!

164
00:05:41,830 --> 00:05:43,300
We got movie sign!

165
00:05:56,410 --> 00:05:57,040
-General Hospital--

166
00:05:57,040 --> 00:05:57,760
-Oh, boy.
 - --is brought to you by--

167
00:05:57,760 --> 00:05:58,260
-Shh!

168
00:05:58,260 --> 00:05:58,830
Quiet you guys.

169
00:05:58,830 --> 00:06:00,080
This is my soap.

170
00:06:00,080 --> 00:06:02,730
I scheduled all my
 classes around it.

171
00:06:02,730 --> 00:06:04,760
-Hey, it's my
 Leonard Cohen album.

172
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:05,260
-Hm!

173
00:06:08,670 --> 00:06:09,730
-God, I'm unappealing.

174
00:06:09,730 --> 00:06:11,690
-Well, anyway, as I
 was saying, Cynthia's

175
00:06:11,690 --> 00:06:13,040
great with the
 kids at the school.

176
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:15,140
I mean, they're all
 crazy about her.

177
00:06:15,140 --> 00:06:15,640
-Hm.

178
00:06:15,640 --> 00:06:17,710
-Oh, I'm sure they are.

179
00:06:17,710 --> 00:06:20,360
-Well, you are very popular
 with the children too, sister.

180
00:06:20,360 --> 00:06:21,020
-Oh.

181
00:06:21,020 --> 00:06:21,820
-Can I take your helmet?

182
00:06:21,820 --> 00:06:24,020
Uh, oh, that's your hair.

183
00:06:24,020 --> 00:06:27,140
-What will you do, Cynthia,
 after you and Ken are married?

184
00:06:27,140 --> 00:06:29,320
Will you go on working
 at the nursery school?

185
00:06:29,320 --> 00:06:30,330
-Oh, I don't know.

186
00:06:30,330 --> 00:06:31,960
I-- I hadn't really
 thought about it.

187
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:32,790
-Oh, that's really smart.

188
00:06:32,790 --> 00:06:33,290
-Mm, hm.

189
00:06:33,290 --> 00:06:36,770
-I haven't talked it
 over with Ken, yet.

190
00:06:36,770 --> 00:06:38,360
-Maybe for a little while?

191
00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:39,320
-Maybe.

192
00:06:39,320 --> 00:06:41,190
Do you think it
 would be a good idea?

193
00:06:41,190 --> 00:06:42,110
[PHONE RINGING]

194
00:06:42,110 --> 00:06:43,040
-I'll get it.

195
00:06:43,040 --> 00:06:44,520
-Oh, it's probably
 the Muzak Corporation

196
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:47,420
and they want their record back.

197
00:06:47,420 --> 00:06:47,920
-Hello.

198
00:06:47,920 --> 00:06:49,310
-Uh, would you
 folks break it up?

199
00:06:49,310 --> 00:06:51,060
You party is depressing
 everyone in the building.

200
00:06:51,060 --> 00:06:52,670
-Hold on.

201
00:06:52,670 --> 00:06:53,920
Cynthia, it's for you.

202
00:06:53,920 --> 00:06:55,810
-Hm.

203
00:06:55,810 --> 00:06:56,830
[TOM HUMMING]

204
00:06:56,830 --> 00:06:58,890
-Student loan people.

205
00:06:58,890 --> 00:07:00,320
-Oh, thank you.

206
00:07:00,320 --> 00:07:04,130
-(SINGING) Whaa, whaa, whaa--

207
00:07:04,130 --> 00:07:04,660
-Yes.

208
00:07:04,660 --> 00:07:05,240
-Oh, that's OK.

209
00:07:05,240 --> 00:07:06,380
We don't have to get married.

210
00:07:06,380 --> 00:07:07,630
I can marry someone else.

211
00:07:07,630 --> 00:07:08,130
-Oh, I see.

212
00:07:08,130 --> 00:07:08,910
Well, that's too bad.

213
00:07:08,910 --> 00:07:09,410
-Uh-huh.

214
00:07:09,410 --> 00:07:10,210
Head injury.
 Mm-hm.

215
00:07:10,210 --> 00:07:10,710
-Oh, no.

216
00:07:10,710 --> 00:07:11,680
Of course it doesn't matter.

217
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:14,150
I understand.

218
00:07:14,150 --> 00:07:15,740
Yes, I can get home all right.

219
00:07:15,740 --> 00:07:17,040
-(WHISPERING) Give
 in to the dark side.

220
00:07:17,040 --> 00:07:17,710
-Will you call me
 in the morning?

221
00:07:17,710 --> 00:07:18,440
-Hm.

222
00:07:18,440 --> 00:07:18,950
Dark side, huh?

223
00:07:18,950 --> 00:07:19,450
-Fine.

224
00:07:19,450 --> 00:07:20,250
-Hm.

225
00:07:20,250 --> 00:07:21,210
Good night, Ken.

226
00:07:21,210 --> 00:07:23,470
-The depressing thing
 is, is this is in color.

227
00:07:23,470 --> 00:07:23,970
-Ah.

228
00:07:23,970 --> 00:07:24,470
-Mm, hm.

229
00:07:24,470 --> 00:07:26,880
-Hm?

230
00:07:26,880 --> 00:07:28,250
-Is that a Cross Your Heart?

231
00:07:28,250 --> 00:07:30,670
-Well, he's at the hospital,
 and he says it doesn't look like

232
00:07:30,670 --> 00:07:31,740
he'll be through--
 [ROBOTS BREATHING HEAVY]

233
00:07:31,740 --> 00:07:32,800
- --in time to come back for me.

234
00:07:32,800 --> 00:07:33,610
[ROBOTS BREATHING HEAVY]

235
00:07:33,610 --> 00:07:35,940
-Well, that's too bad.

236
00:07:35,940 --> 00:07:38,090
-Well, can I, uh, warm up
 your bra--uh, c-coffee?

237
00:07:38,090 --> 00:07:40,940
- --coming by and
 pick you up, at all.

238
00:07:40,940 --> 00:07:43,100
-I'm afraid not.

239
00:07:43,100 --> 00:07:44,010
-Hm.

240
00:07:44,010 --> 00:07:46,450
-Aw, it's a shame it
 had to be this way.

241
00:07:46,450 --> 00:07:48,390
Well, tonight of all nights.

242
00:07:48,390 --> 00:07:50,240
-I'm sorry too.

243
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:52,000
-When Anne Sexton
 throws a party--

244
00:07:52,000 --> 00:07:55,050
-Well, it's getting late and--
 and since Ken isn't coming

245
00:07:55,050 --> 00:07:55,550
back--

246
00:07:55,550 --> 00:07:56,900
-And since I'm
 ruining your marriage!

247
00:07:56,900 --> 00:07:57,830
-I gues I'd better go.

248
00:07:57,830 --> 00:08:00,370
-Well, Cynthia, can I give you
 some more coffee, or some cake,

249
00:08:00,370 --> 00:08:00,870
or somethign?

250
00:08:00,870 --> 00:08:02,120
-Oh, like that
 solves everything!

251
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:02,620
-No, thanks.

252
00:08:02,620 --> 00:08:04,860
Really.

253
00:08:04,860 --> 00:08:06,420
I'll just, uh, call a cab.

254
00:08:06,420 --> 00:08:07,520
-Well, wait a minute.

255
00:08:07,520 --> 00:08:08,630
Why take a cab?

256
00:08:08,630 --> 00:08:09,980
I can drive you home.

257
00:08:09,980 --> 00:08:10,670
-Oh, no.

258
00:08:10,670 --> 00:08:12,220
Phil, there's no reason
 for you to do that.

259
00:08:12,220 --> 00:08:14,170
-Well I'm not going to
 let you go home in a cab.

260
00:08:14,170 --> 00:08:15,910
-I don't like to put
 you to any trouble.

261
00:08:15,910 --> 00:08:16,900
-It's no trouble.

262
00:08:16,900 --> 00:08:17,400
